<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ethiopia is a country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> with one of the longest continuous state traditions in the world. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, fought a long war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896 before being occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> from 1936 to 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopia.jpg" alt="Ethiopia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian-Adal War drew in both global rivals of the age: about 400 Portuguese musketeers fought for <b>Ethiopia</b>, while Ottoman musketeers fought for Adal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centres are traditionally placed at Mareeg in central Somalia and at Qalaafo on the upper Shabelle, in what is now <b>Ethiopia</b>’s Somali Region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aksumite Empire emerged in the highlands of northern <b>Ethiopia</b> and Eritrea around the first century CE, though its roots extend to the pre-Aksumite civilisation that had absorbed South Arabian cultural and linguistic influences centuries earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Succession was matrilineal, passing to the son of the king's sister rather than to his own son, and Abu al-Makarim may describe a mobile royal encampment of the kind later known in the Funj sultanate, <b>Ethiopia</b> and Darfur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ethiopia</b> became the Soviet bloc's closest ally in Africa and one of the best-armed states of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ethiopia</b>'s survival in 1543 owed much to some 400 Portuguese musketeers, whose firepower helped Emperor Galawdewos defeat and kill Ahmad Gran at Wayna Daga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
